Both are testing patterns but serve different purposes.

```
PAGE OBJECT MODEL:
├─ Encapsulates page structure
├─ Holds locators and methods
├─ Reduces code duplication
└─ Improves maintainability

FIXTURES:
├─ Provides setup/teardown
├─ Shares test data/contexts
├─ Reduces duplication
└─ Improves test reusability
```

**Answer:**
```
"Page Object Model (POM) encapsulates UI elements and 
interactions for a specific page:

// pages/LoginPage.js
export class LoginPage {
    constructor(page) {
        this.page = page;
        this.emailInput = page.locator('[data-testid="email"]');
        this.submitButton = page.locator('button[type="submit"]');
    }
    async login(email, password) {
        await this.emailInput.fill(email);
        await this.submitButton.click();
    }
}

Fixtures provide reusable test setup:

const test = base.extend({
    authenticatedPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
        // Login setup
        const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);
        await loginPage.login('user@example.com', 'password');
        
        // Pass to test
        await use(page);
        
        // Cleanup
        await page.goto('/logout');
    }
});

Use POM for UI abstraction, fixtures for test setup."
```

